  "narrative": "All future estimates will be interpreted as already signed to the registered hypothesis direction. For direction -1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means stronger dependency in the exposed group; for direction +1 main effects, a positive signed estimate means weaker dependency. For interaction claims, a positive signed estimate means the signed main effect is larger in the focal subgroup than in the complement. These remain associations in the supplied dataset, not causal or generalizable claims.\n\nThe strongest supported findings are now SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency, MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency, KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency, and PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. Further confirmation of those main effects would add little value. The next scientifically useful questions are whether these accepted effects are modified by cellular states, and whether some rejected or near-null oncogene-addiction effects become meaningful in cleaner co-mutation contexts.\n\nI propose four context-modification interactions for the accepted core effects. First, PTEN loss and STAT3 dependency may be modified by EMT state: STAT3 is linked to invasive and cytokine-associated survival signaling, so EMT may amplify the PTEN-loss STAT3 dependency effect. The registered direction remains -1, and a positive interaction would mean the PTEN-loss stronger-dependency effect is larger in high-EMT lines. Second, KEAP1 loss and NFE2L2 dependency may be modified by hypoxia, because both are connected to oxidative stress and metabolic adaptation. The accepted KEAP1 effect is direction +1, so a positive interaction would mean the weaker-dependency effect of KEAP1 loss is larger in high-hypoxia lines. Third, SMARCA4-loss synthetic lethality with SMARCA2 may depend on transcriptional state, proxied by MYC expression; high MYC expression could alter chromatin-remodeling dependency, but SMARCA2 dependency may be highly specific to SMARCA4 loss, making null interaction plausible. Fourth, MSI-high and WRN dependency may be modified by immune-associated IFN-gamma signature. This tests whether the WRN effect is embedded in immune-state biology; if null, the association is likely more genomic than immune context dependent.\n\nI also propose two refinement tests for apparent oncogene-addiction nulls. EGFR amplification has not supported stronger EGFR dependency overall or within KRAS wild-type lines. One reason a true oncogene dependency could be masked is co-occurring bypass mutations. By requiring both KRAS wild-type and PIK3CA wild-type, we ask whether EGFR dependency becomes visible in a cleaner driver-negative context. If this is still null, the EGFR-dependence signal can be considered weakly supported or absent in this dataset across plausible contexts. The same logic applies to ERBB2 amplification, refined within KRAS wild-type and PIK3CA wild-type lines.\n\nFinally, I propose a specificity test for SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss lines. ARID1A loss alone did not support stronger SMARCA2 dependency, and the interaction between SMARCA4 loss and ARID1A loss has not provided strong evidence of context modification. Measuring ARID1A loss within SMARCA4-loss lines asks a narrower question: after SMARCA4 loss, does co-occurring ARID1A loss further change SMARCA2 dependency? This can clarify whether the main SMARCA4-SMARCA2 association is specific to SMARCA4-loss state or sensitive to additional SWI/SNF subunit loss. The direction is registered as stronger dependency, but expectation is uncertain.",
  "research_notes": "Round-8 explore update. Signed convention remains: direction -1 positive = stronger dependency; direction +1 positive = weaker dependency; interaction positive = larger signed main effect in focal subgroup. Current core accepted associations: SMARCA4 loss with stronger SMARCA2 dependency; MSI-high with stronger WRN dependency; KEAP1 loss with weaker NFE2L2 dependency; PTEN loss with stronger STAT3 dependency. These are dataset associations, not causal/generalizable claims. Negative or unsupported: EGFR amp/EGFR, ERBB2 amp/ERBB2, BRAF V600E/BRAF, KRAS mut/KRAS, KRAS mut/EGFR, PIK3CA mut/ERBB2 or KRAS, MYC amp across many outcomes, RB1 loss/POLR2A or KIF18A, TP53 loss/RPL11 or MDM2, CDKN2A loss/KIF18A, ARID1A loss/SMARCA2 overall, NF1 loss/KRAS, MTAP loss/POLR2A, constructed marker screens. Context tests mostly null so far: high TMB x MSI/WRN, low 9p21 x SMARCA4/SMARCA2, signature A x KEAP1/NFE2L2, serum x PTEN/STAT3 still uncertain, Cas9 interactions no clear positive.\n\nRegistered round-8 proposals target: 1) EMT high x PTEN/STAT3 interaction; 2) hypoxia high x KEAP1/NFE2L2 interaction; 3) MYC expression high x SMARCA4/SMARCA2 interaction; 4) IFN-gamma high x MSI/WRN interaction; 5) EGFR amp vs EGFR dependency within KRAS wildtype and PIK3CA wildtype; 6) ERBB2 amp vs ERBB2 dependency within KRAS wildtype and PIK3CA wildtype; 7) ARID1A loss vs SMARCA2 dependency within SMARCA4-loss lines. Active untested H111-H115 remain culture-context robustness interactions plus PTEN loss vs ERBB2. Next analyze should select among H111-H115 and new context/context specificity proposals depending on cell counts and prior evidence. No validation requested yet; four new validation requests remain. Acceptance threshold still requires a reasonably large precise signed effect; context interaction would be meaningful if it changes accepted effects by a nontrivial fraction of their already-large main effects."
